# {{ ansible_managed }}
# =============================================================================
# Curated alarm — swap utilisation (ADR-030, phase 1)
# =============================================================================
# The FIRST curated alarm, and it is deliberately the one the observability
# README uses as its own cautionary tale: Netdata's stock `used_swap` sat
# CRITICAL for 5 days 19 hours in August 2026 and reached no one, because it
# addresses `sysadmin` and no role is routed.
#
# This one carries the SAME condition and the SAME chart, with the threshold
# already chosen and justified in `homelab-health.sh`. The stock alarm is left
# exactly as it is — still evaluating, still silent — so nothing that exists
# today changes.
#
# `to: silent` is not an oversight, it is the design. Netdata notifies nobody,
# here as everywhere else. The state of this alarm is READ by the host-side
# adapter (homelab-netdata-kuma.sh) and pushed into its own Uptime Kuma monitor,
# which keeps Kuma the single place the notification channel is configured — and
# makes the monitor a dead-man's switch for netdata itself, which a
# transition-driven notification could never be.
#
# The `alarm:` name below is the contract with that adapter. Renaming it without
# updating netdata_curated_alarms makes the monitor go red saying the alarm is
# not loaded, which is the intended failure: loud, not silent.
#
# Threshold honesty, carried over verbatim from homelab-health.sh: 85 % is a
# STARTING POINT, not a measured threshold. Occupancy accumulates cold pages
# until something restarts the containers, so it is not a steady state to
# project from. Hysteresis clears at 5 points below so a value sitting on the
# line cannot flap.
#
# `calc` is the stock formula, copied rather than reinvented, including its
# divide-by-zero guard.
#
# `| int` is not decoration. A variable set in group_vars arrives as an integer
# because YAML parses it, but the same variable passed with `-e` on the command
# line arrives as a STRING, and `"30" - 5` aborts the deploy. That is how the
# threshold is overridden for a deliberate fire test, so the untyped form worked
# for months of normal deploys and failed the first time it mattered.
#
# PHASE 1 DELETES NOTHING. `homelab-health.sh` still carries its own swap
# check; the two overlap on purpose until this one has been observed firing.
# =============================================================================

    alarm: homelab_used_swap
       on: mem.swap
    class: Utilization
     type: System
component: Memory
     calc: (($used + $free) > 0) ? ($used * 100 / ($used + $free)) : 0
    units: %
    every: 1m
     warn: $this > (($status >= $WARNING) ? ({{ netdata_swap_threshold_pct | int - 5 }}) : ({{ netdata_swap_threshold_pct | int }}))
    delay: up 2m down 15m multiplier 1.5 max 1h
  summary: Homelab swap utilisation above {{ netdata_swap_threshold_pct }}%
     info: Swap utilisation crossed the threshold justified in homelab-health.sh
       to: silent
